\chapter{S-expressions, E-graphs and Equality
Saturation}\label{ch32-s-expressions-e-graphs-and-equality-saturation}

This chapter builds the workflow from rule text to equality-triggered
rewriting. Its APIs exist in \texttt{dsl:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}} in
C++ and at module scope in D.

\section{1. Construct and read the interchange
language}\label{ch32-construct-and-read-the-interchange-language}

An \texttt{SExpr} has \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}atom}, \texttt{text},
\texttt{items}, and \texttt{quoted} fields. Atoms carry text; lists
carry expressions. Prefer the \texttt{atom}/\texttt{list} factories,
which establish the appropriate representation and quoting. D
expressions deeply copy their mutable list storage; C++ expressions have
ordinary vector value semantics.

\begin{code}
auto expression = dsl::SExpr::list({
    dsl::SExpr::atom("add"), dsl::SExpr::atom("a"), dsl::SExpr::atom("0")});
assert(expression.dump() == "(add a 0)");
auto parsed = dsl::parse_sexpr("; comment\n(add a 0)");
assert(parsed == expression);
\end{code}

\begin{code}
auto expression = SExpr.list([SExpr.atom("add"), SExpr.atom("a"), SExpr.atom("0")]);
assert(expression.dump() == "(add a 0)");
assert(parse_sexpr("; comment\n(add a 0)") == expression);
\end{code}

The notation is:

\begin{code}
expression = atom | quoted_atom | "(" , { expression } , ")" ;
quoted_atom = '"' , { character | escape } , '"' ;
escape = "\\n" | "\\r" | "\\t" | '\\"' | "\\\\" ;
\end{code}

Whitespace separates unquoted atoms; parentheses delimit lists; a
semicolon starts a comment through the end of the line. Empty lists and
quoted empty atoms are valid S-expressions. Quotedness is retained.
\texttt{dump(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} quotes atoms as necessary and
escapes special characters. The reader bounds recursive nesting at 512
levels and rejects malformed lists/escapes.

\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}sexpr} requires exactly one expression.
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}sexpressions} returns all expressions in a
source, including an empty sequence for empty input.

\section{2. Understand tree
conversion}\label{ch32-understand-tree-conversion}

\texttt{ast\_\allowbreak{}to\_\allowbreak{}sexpr} maps a leaf to
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}tag value)\allowbreak{}} and an inner node to
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}tag child1 child\allowbreak{}2 .\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}.
\texttt{sexpr\_\allowbreak{}to\_\allowbreak{}ast} maps a list with one
atom argument to a leaf, other headed lists to inner nodes, and a bare
atom to tag \texttt{atom}. An operator head must be an atom. That
convention cannot distinguish an inner node with one bare-atom child
from a leaf payload; retain \texttt{SExpr} when the interchange model
itself is the desired syntax tree.

An e-graph accepts an \texttt{ASTNode} through
\texttt{add(\allowbreak{}tree)\allowbreak{}} and produces one through
\texttt{extract\_\allowbreak{}ast(\allowbreak{}id)\allowbreak{}} using
these conventions.

\section{3. Declare equality rules}\label{ch32-declare-equality-rules}

\begin{code}
(rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
(rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
(= (add ?left ?right) (add ?right ?left))
\end{code}

\texttt{sexpr\_\allowbreak{}rule} reads one rule;
\texttt{sexpr\_\allowbreak{}rules} reads a sequence. Each
\texttt{ERewriteRule} stores \texttt{name}, \texttt{lhs}, and
\texttt{rhs}. Shorthand \texttt{=} and
\texttt{=\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}} rules use the operator text as
their name.

An unquoted atom starting with \texttt{?} and at least one following
character is a variable. Quoted \texttt{"?x"} is a literal. Variables
may occupy complete term positions, including the entire LHS, but
operator heads are literal atoms. Every RHS variable must occur in the
LHS. Empty lists and list-valued heads are invalid e-graph terms even
though they are valid general S-expressions.

A rule's search orientation is LHS to RHS. Applying it records equality
in both directions. Supply a reverse-oriented rule when that reverse
pattern should independently trigger a search.

\section{4. Add terms and establish
equality}\label{ch32-add-terms-and-establish-equality}

\begin{code}
dsl::EGraph graph;
auto a = graph.add("a");
auto b = graph.add("b");
auto fa = graph.add("(f a)");
auto fb = graph.add("(f b)");
assert(!graph.equivalent(fa, fb));
graph.unite(a, b);
assert(graph.equivalent(fa, fb));
\end{code}

Use \texttt{new EGraph} in D; the remaining calls have the same
spelling.

An e-node records an operator, atom/quoted status, and child e-class
IDs. Hash-consing shares nodes with the same canonical children.
Union-find stores equivalence classes; \texttt{unite} marks the graph
for congruence rebuilding. \texttt{rebuild} discovers parent
equivalences and removes duplicate canonical nodes. Queries such as
\texttt{equivalent}, \texttt{matches}, counts, and extraction rebuild
first. \texttt{find} returns an ID's current representative. IDs stay
valid after merging.

\texttt{node\_\allowbreak{}count(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} counts
retained unique e-nodes, whereas
\texttt{class\_\allowbreak{}count(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} counts
equivalence classes. A class can retain several nodes/alternatives.

\section{5. Match by equality}\label{ch32-match-by-equality}

\begin{code}
graph.add("(pair a b)");
auto found = graph.matches(dsl::parse_sexpr("(pair ?x ?x)"));
assert(found.size() == 1); // a and b were merged above
\end{code}

\begin{code}
auto graph = new EGraph;
auto a = graph.add("a"), b = graph.add("b");
graph.unite(a, b);
graph.add("(pair a b)");
auto found = graph.matches(parse_sexpr("(pair ?x ?x)"));
assert(found.length == 1);
\end{code}

\texttt{EMatch.\allowbreak{}root} identifies the matched class;
\texttt{bindings} maps variable names to IDs. Matching explores
alternatives within classes. Repeated variables compare canonical class
IDs, so equality is sufficient even when textual leaves differ.
\texttt{matches(\allowbreak{}pattern,\allowbreak{} limit)\allowbreak{}}
bounds the number of returned records.

\section{6. Saturate without discarding
alternatives}\label{ch32-saturate-without-discarding-alternatives}

\texttt{saturate(\allowbreak{}rules,\allowbreak{} limits,\allowbreak{} observer)\allowbreak{}}
performs iterations of e-matching, RHS instantiation, equality merging
and rebuilding. It reaches saturation when an iteration produces no
graph revision. New RHS forms remain available to later rules and to
later iterations. The original forms remain represented too.

\begin{code}
dsl::EGraph graph;
auto root = graph.add("(mul (add a 0) 1)");
auto report = graph.saturate(dsl::sexpr_rules(R"(
    (rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)
    (rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)
)"));
assert(report.saturated());
assert(graph.extract(root).dump() == "a");
\end{code}

\texttt{SaturationLimits\allowbreak{}} fields/defaults are:

{\def\LTcaptype{none} % do not increment counter
\begin{longtable}[]{@{}lrl@{}}
\toprule\noalign{}
Field & Default & Bound \\
\midrule\noalign{}
\endhead
\bottomrule\noalign{}
\endlastfoot
\texttt{iterations} & 30 & Number of saturation iterations \\
\texttt{nodes} & 100000 & Retained graph node budget during insertion \\
\texttt{matches} & 100000 & Total successful rule applications \\
\end{longtable}
}

\texttt{SaturationResult\allowbreak{}} exposes \texttt{iterations},
\texttt{applications}, \texttt{stop}, and
\texttt{saturated(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}. \texttt{stop} is
\texttt{Saturated}, \texttt{IterationLimit}, \texttt{NodeLimit}, or
\texttt{MatchLimit}. Partial results remain valid after a bounded stop;
an interrupted RHS insertion can leave newly inserted subterms without
merging its unfinished parent. The temporary node budget is restored
when the run returns.

\section{7. Trigger user rewriters}\label{ch32-trigger-user-rewriters}

\texttt{when\_\allowbreak{}equal} performs an explicit equality check
and calls the supplied rewriter with the graph and canonical
representative:

\begin{code}
auto atom = graph.add("a");
graph.when_equal(root, atom, [](dsl::EGraph& g, dsl::EClassId id) {
    auto tree = g.extract_ast(id);
    // Apply domain-specific AST transformations or visitor operations here.
    assert(tree.tag() == "atom");
});
\end{code}

\begin{code}
auto atom = graph.add("a");
graph.when_equal(root, atom, (EGraph g, EClassId id) {
    auto tree = g.extract_ast(id);
    assert(tree.tag() == "atom");
});
\end{code}

The return value says whether the callback ran. Each call checks current
equality; this is a direct invocation API. For observing saturation
itself, its third argument receives
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}ERewriteRule,\allowbreak{} EMatch)\allowbreak{}}
after each completed application. An application may report an
already-established equality, and a binding may be observed again in
subsequent iterations. Keep observer accounting separate from the
graph-change criterion for saturation.

\section{8. Extract a finite
representative}\label{ch32-extract-a-finite-representative}

\texttt{extract(\allowbreak{}id)\allowbreak{}} computes minimum node
counts over the class alternatives, then constructs an \texttt{SExpr}.
Cyclic alternatives do not force a cyclic output when a finite
representative exists. For example, merging \texttt{a} with
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}wrap a)\allowbreak{}} still permits extracting
\texttt{a}. There is no user-supplied extraction cost model in the
current API; the implemented metric is node count.

The integration examples linked from Chapter 30 combine rule
preprocessing, saturation, equality-triggered visitors and grammar
recognition. Chapters 34 and 35 explain the visitor and preprocessing
stages in detail.
